🔧 What Needs Refinement
1. Missing the “Why” Behind the Progress Page
We need to anchor this PRD in our product’s secret: shortening the feedback loop. Right now, it reads like a great analytics dashboard, but not a co-pilot that learns and nudges.
Add a “Purpose” section at the top: What decisions will this page help users make today, this week, and over time?


Reframe "Progress" as a feedback system — not just display but loop-closure.


📌 Example: Instead of just showing “hydration was low on 3 days,” the system should say “On days you drank <5 cups, constipation severity was higher. Add a hydration streak goal?”

2. From Analytics to Agency
The current design stops short at observation. We must push toward recommendation, habit suggestion, or copilot guidance.


Every card must end in:


a “Suggested Habit”,


an “Add to Tracker”, or


a “CoPilot Ask” (e.g., “Ask me why bloating spiked Wednesday”).



3. Visualization Needs Clinical & User Alignment
“Gut Score” is ambiguous. Is it symptom severity? Balance of all metrics? A risk score?


Let’s define a multi-dimensional score or drop it altogether in favor of "Trends You Should Know."


Add “Download for Doctor” and “Pattern Summary for Clinician” buttons.


Show export formats we support, how they align with clinical codes (e.g., Rome IV, Bristol Stool Chart), and ICD/AI-supported formats if possible.



4. Trigger → Insight → Action Loop Isn’t Closed
Each card should follow this cycle:
Trigger: "You’ve logged X times this week"


Insight: "This pattern is associated with Y symptom"


Action: "Try Z habit, Copilot will check in Friday"


We need to model this logic per card.

5. Redundancy Across Sections
There’s some repetition between the Card Design, AI Insight Types, and Agentic Flow sections. Consider:
Merging AI Insight Types with Agentic Flow — show how agents power each card.


Creating a visual or table mapping cards → agents → insights → recommended actions.



6. Missing: Feedback/Reflection Loop
This page should allow users to reflect or respond:
“Did this insight resonate with you?” 👍👎


“Would you like to add this habit?”


“Did this help you poop better this week?” → micro-NPS.


This feedback can go into improving insight accuracy (RLHF-like) and closing the loop.

7. Language Needs Compliance Check
Flag any claims that could be interpreted as diagnostic or therapeutic. Use phrasing like:
“Patterns suggest…”


“You might consider…”


“Some users with similar patterns have…”


Let’s make sure the Compliance Check Agent is embedded in every card’s insight generator.

✅ Suggested Next Steps
Add a Purpose Statement at the top.


Map each card to: agent → insight → habit/call-to-action.


Draft a few example user journeys (e.g., user with bloating + poor sleep + high stress — what would they see after 7 days?).


Include feedback options and copilot nudges in every card.


Tighten up language for medical compliance.



Thanks again — this is a really solid v1. Let’s make it truly copilot-grade in v2.


Below in more detail:

Product Requirements Document (PRD)



Overview
This document outlines the redesigned architecture for the Gutsphere Progress Page, transitioning it from a retrospective, data-heavy layout to a dynamic, agent-driven system that closes the most important feedback loops in a GI patient's journey. Every insight must drive action and every data view should be multidisciplinary, interactive, and longitudinal.

Core Objective
To turn the Progress Page into a true co-pilot that:
Closes symptom and treatment feedback loops


Surfaces root causes and subtle patterns


Helps users navigate clinical care (diagnostics + preparation)


Promotes prevention, performance, and gut microbiome rebuilding



